## Migration: `Thread.Abort` → Cooperative Cancellation (.NET 8)

### Why this breaks

In .NET 6+, `Thread.Abort()` throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` — it simply doesn't work. `ThreadAbortException` is never raised, `Thread.ResetAbort()` is obsolete (`SYSLIB0006`).

---

### Pattern Classification

| Usage | Pattern | Replacement |
|-------|---------|-------------|
| `_running` loop + `Thread.Abort()` | Cancellable work loop | `CancellationToken` |
| `catch (ThreadAbortException)` | Control flow cleanup | `catch (OperationCanceledException)` |
| `Thread.ResetAbort()` | Continue after abort | `token.IsCancellationRequested` check |
| `GetNextItem()` blocks | Blocking call interruption | Token-aware wait |

---

### Migrated Code

```csharp
public class BackgroundProcessor : IDisposable
{
    private Thread? _workerThread;
    private CancellationTokenSource? _cts;

    public void Start()
    {
        _cts = new CancellationTokenSource();
        _workerThread = new Thread(() => ProcessLoop(_cts.Token));
        _workerThread.IsBackground = true;
        _workerThread.Start();
    }

    private void ProcessLoop(CancellationToken token)
    {
        while (!token.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            try
            {
                var item = GetNextItem(token); // pass token to unblock
                ProcessItem(item, token);
            }
            catch (OperationCanceledException) when (token.IsCancellationRequested)
            {
                // Equivalent to the old ThreadAbortException handler
                RollbackCurrentTransaction();
                // No ResetAbort needed — we control whether to continue or exit
                break;
            }
            finally
            {
                FlushBuffers();
            }
        }
    }

    public void Stop()
    {
        if (_cts is null) return;

        _cts.Cancel(); // signal cooperative cancellation

        // Give the thread time to finish gracefully
        _workerThread?.Join(TimeSpan.FromSeconds(2));

        // If still alive after timeout, the thread is truly stuck.
        // There is NO equivalent to Thread.Abort in modern .NET.
        // Options: log a warning, or move uncooperative work to a child process.
    }

    public void Dispose()
    {
        Stop();
        _cts?.Dispose();
    }
}
```

---

### Key changes to `GetNextItem`

The blocking `GetNextItem()` **must** be made cancellation-aware, otherwise `Cancel()` won't interrupt it:

```csharp
// Before (.NET Framework) — blocks indefinitely
private WorkItem GetNextItem()
{
    return _queue.Take(); // blocks until item available
}

// After (.NET 8) — unblocks on cancellation
private WorkItem GetNextItem(CancellationToken token)
{
    return _queue.Take(token); // throws OperationCanceledException when cancelled
    // Or if using a custom wait:
    // token.WaitHandle.WaitOne(timeout) instead of Thread.Sleep
}
```

---

### Critical Pitfalls

| Pitfall | Why it matters |
|---------|---------------|
| **Not passing token to blocking calls** | `_cts.Cancel()` only works if `GetNextItem` checks the token. Without it, the thread hangs forever — worse than the original. |
| **Using `Thread.Join()` without timeout** | If the worker never exits, `Stop()` deadlocks. Always use `Join(timeout)`. |
| **Not disposing `CancellationTokenSource`** | Leaks timer handles. Implement `IDisposable`. |
| **Swallowing `OperationCanceledException`** | Use `when (token.IsCancellationRequested)` to distinguish cancellation from other failures. |
| **Expecting immediate stop** | Cooperative cancellation only fires at checkpoints. If `ProcessItem` is long-running, add `token.ThrowIfCancellationRequested()` inside it. |
| **Losing the cleanup logic** | The old `ThreadAbortException` catch did `RollbackCurrentTransaction()` — don't forget to preserve this in the new `OperationCanceledException` handler or a `finally` block. |

---

### If `GetNextItem` cannot be modified

If you can't change the blocking call (third-party, native code), your only recourse in modern .NET is to run it in a **separate process** and use `Process.Kill()` — there is no in-process forced termination.
